from common import *
mc('R3.1','sl','Recognizing a conjugate pair','Which pair is a Brønsted–Lowry conjugate acid–base pair?', ['H₂SO₄ and SO₄²⁻','H₂CO₃ and HCO₃⁻','NH₄⁺ and NH₂⁻','H₃O⁺ and OH⁻'],1,'A conjugate pair differs by exactly one proton. H₂CO₃ loses one H⁺ to form HCO₃⁻; the other pairs differ by two protons.')
mc('R3.1','sl','A strong-acid dilution',r'$10.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl is diluted to $100.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. Assume complete dissociation and negligible water contribution. What is the pH?', ['1.00','2.00','3.00','4.00'],2,'The tenfold dilution gives [H⁺]=0.00100 mol dm⁻³ and pH=−log₁₀(0.00100)=3.00.')
mc('R3.1','sl','An amphiprotic species','Which species can both accept and donate a proton in appropriate aqueous reactions?', ['CO₃²⁻','HCO₃⁻','Na⁺','Cl⁻'],1,'HCO₃⁻ can gain H⁺ to form H₂CO₃ or lose H⁺ to form CO₃²⁻.')
mc('R3.1','ahl','Conjugate strength',r'An acid has $K_a=2.00\times10^{-5}$ at a temperature where $K_w=1.00\times10^{-14}$. What is $K_b$ of its conjugate base?', [r'$2.00\times10^{-19}$',r'$5.00\times10^{-10}$',r'$5.00\times10^{8}$',r'$2.00\times10^{-5}$'],1,'For a conjugate pair KaKb=Kw, so Kb=10⁻¹⁴/(2.00×10⁻⁵)=5.00×10⁻¹⁰.')
mc('R3.1','ahl','Selecting a buffer','Which mixture forms an acidic buffer after reaction? All listed amounts are in moles.', ['0.10 HCl and 0.10 NaCl','0.10 CH₃COOH and 0.04 NaOH','0.10 CH₃COOH and 0.20 NaOH','0.10 HCl and 0.04 NaOH'],1,'Partial neutralization leaves 0.06 mol weak acid and produces 0.04 mol conjugate base. A buffer requires appreciable amounts of both members of a weak conjugate pair.')
mc('R3.2','sl','Identifying the reducing agent',r'For $\mathrm{2Fe^{2+}+Cl_2\rightarrow2Fe^{3+}+2Cl^-}$, which species is the reducing agent?', ['Fe²⁺','Fe³⁺','Cl₂','Cl⁻'],0,'Fe²⁺ loses an electron and is oxidized, supplying the electrons that reduce chlorine.')
mc('R3.2','sl','Molten salt electrolysis','Molten MgCl₂ is electrolysed using inert electrodes. Which products form at cathode and anode respectively?', ['Mg and Cl₂','H₂ and O₂','Cl₂ and Mg','Mg and O₂'],0,'Mg²⁺ gains electrons at the cathode to form Mg; Cl⁻ loses electrons at the anode to form Cl₂. There is no water in the molten electrolyte.')
mc('R3.2','ahl','A standard cell potential',r'Standard reduction potentials are $E^\circ(\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag})=+0.80\ \mathrm{V}$ and $E^\circ(\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu})=+0.34\ \mathrm{V}$. What is the standard potential for the spontaneous cell?', ['+1.14 V','+0.46 V','−0.46 V','+0.12 V'],1,'Ag⁺ is reduced at the cathode and Cu is oxidized at the anode. E°cell=0.80−0.34=+0.46 V; electrode potentials are not multiplied by balancing coefficients.')
mc('R3.3','sl','Radical initiation','Which equation is an initiation step in the photochemical chlorination of an alkane?', ['Cl₂ → 2Cl·','CH₄ + Cl· → CH₃· + HCl','CH₃· + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl·','CH₃· + Cl· → CH₃Cl'],0,'Light-induced homolysis of Cl₂ creates radicals from a non-radical molecule. The next two are propagation; the last is termination.')
mc('R3.3','sl','A chain termination event','Which event terminates a radical chain?', ['A radical reacts with a molecule and produces a new radical','Two radicals combine to give a molecule without an unpaired electron','A covalent bond undergoes homolysis','Light excites a molecule before bond cleavage'],1,'Termination removes radicals without generating replacement radicals. Radical–radical combination is a standard termination event.')
mc('R3.4','sl','Electron-pair movement','In nucleophilic substitution of a halogenoalkane by OH⁻, where should a curly arrow representing attack begin?', ['At the positive region on carbon','At an oxygen lone pair','At the hydrogen bonded to oxygen','At the formal negative charge symbol with no associated electrons'],1,'A curly arrow tracks electron-pair movement, so it begins at the oxygen lone pair and points toward the electrophilic carbon.')
mc('R3.4','sl','Adding bromine to an alkene','What is the organic product of adding Br₂ across the double bond of propene under ordinary addition conditions?', ['1-bromopropane','2-bromopropane','1,2-dibromopropane','1,3-dibromopropane'],2,'One bromine adds to each of the two alkene carbons, forming CH₂BrCHBrCH₃.')
mc('R3.4','ahl','A Lewis acid–base adduct',r'In formation of $\mathrm{H_3N\rightarrow BF_3}$, which species is the Lewis acid?', ['NH₃, because it donates a proton','NH₃, because it donates an electron pair','BF₃, because it accepts an electron pair','BF₃, because it donates an electron pair'],2,'BF₃ accepts the nitrogen lone pair into an electron-deficient boron centre. NH₃ is the Lewis base.')
mc('R3.4','ahl','Substitution at a chiral carbon','A substitution is known to proceed exclusively by an SN2 mechanism at a stereogenic carbon. What stereochemical change is expected at that centre?', ['Retention only','Inversion','A planar carbocation followed by complete racemization','Loss of every stereogenic centre in the molecule'],1,'Backside attack with simultaneous leaving-group departure inverts the arrangement at the reacting centre. A free planar carbocation is not an SN2 intermediate.')
wr('R3.1','sl','Proton transfer in water',r'Consider $\mathrm{NH_3(aq)+H_2O(l)\rightleftharpoons NH_4^+(aq)+OH^-(aq)}$.',[('Identify the acid, base and their conjugate partners in the forward direction.',[('A1','H₂O is the acid; OH⁻ is its conjugate base.'),('A1','NH₃ is the base; NH₄⁺ is its conjugate acid.')]),('Explain why a dilute ammonia solution can be basic even though NH₃ contains no hydroxide ion in its formula.',[('R1','NH₃ accepts a proton from water.'),('R1','That proton transfer forms OH⁻ in solution, increasing hydroxide concentration.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('R3.1','sl','Mixing a strong acid and base',r'$30.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl is mixed with $20.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. Assume additive volumes, complete dissociation and negligible water contribution.',[('Determine the excess reagent and its amount after reaction.',[('M1','Initial H⁺=0.00300 mol and OH⁻=0.00200 mol; they react 1:1.'),('A1','H⁺ is in excess by 0.00100 mol.')]),('Calculate the final pH.',[('M1','[H⁺]=0.00100/0.0500=0.0200 mol dm⁻³.'),('A1','pH=−log₁₀(0.0200)=1.70.')]),('Explain why averaging the initial pH values is not a valid method.',[('R1','Neutralization changes the amounts of H⁺ and OH⁻, and pH is logarithmic rather than a quantity that can be volume-averaged directly.')])])
wr('R3.1','sl','A strong-acid titration curve','The graph is constructed for titration of 25.00 cm³ of a strong monoprotic acid with 0.1000 mol dm⁻³ NaOH at 298 K. Volumes are additive.',[('Read the equivalence volume and determine the acid concentration.',[('A1','Equivalence occurs at 25.0 cm³.'),('M1','At equivalence n(acid)=n(NaOH)=0.1000×0.02500.'),('A1','Acid concentration=0.1000 mol dm⁻³.')]),('Explain the pH at equivalence and why the curve is steep near this point.',[('R1','At 298 K the strong-acid/strong-base salt does not appreciably hydrolyse, so the solution is approximately neutral at pH 7.'),('R1','A small added volume switches the solution from a small excess of H⁺ to a small excess of OH⁻, causing a large logarithmic pH change.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(0,1),(5,1.176),(10,1.368),(20,1.954),(24,2.690),(24.9,3.698),(25,7),(25.1,10.300),(26,11.292),(30,11.959),(40,12.363)],'#186c93')],'NaOH added / cm³','pH',ylim=(0,14),title='Constructed strong-acid/strong-base titration',xticks=[0,5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40],yticks=[0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14]))
wr('R3.1','ahl','A weak-acid calculation',r'A monoprotic acid HA has $K_a=1.80\times10^{-5}$ and initial concentration $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ at 298 K. Assume dissociation is small.',[('Estimate [H⁺] and pH, showing your approximation.',[('M1','Ka=x²/(0.100−x)≈x²/0.100.'),('A1','[H⁺]=√(1.80×10⁻⁶)=1.34×10⁻³ mol dm⁻³.'),('A1','pH=2.87.')]),('Calculate the percentage dissociation and assess the approximation.',[('A1','Percentage dissociation=(0.0013416/0.100)×100=1.34%.'),('R1','This is small compared with 100% (and below 5%), so neglecting x in the denominator is reasonable.')])])
wr('R3.1','ahl','A buffer after adding alkali',r'A buffer contains $0.0600\ \mathrm{mol}$ HA and $0.0400\ \mathrm{mol}$ A⁻, with $pK_a=4.80$. Then $0.0100\ \mathrm{mol}$ NaOH is added in negligible volume. Assume ideal behaviour.',[('Calculate the initial pH using the weak-acid equilibrium expression.',[('M1',r'$[\mathrm{H^+}]=K_a[\mathrm{HA}]/[\mathrm{A^-}]$, or $\mathrm{pH}=pK_a+\log([\mathrm{A^-}]/[\mathrm{HA}])$.'),('A1','Initial pH=4.80+log(0.0400/0.0600)=4.62.')]),('Write the reaction with OH⁻ and calculate the new pH.',[('A1','HA+OH⁻→A⁻+H₂O.'),('M1','After reaction: HA=0.0500 mol and A⁻=0.0500 mol.'),('A1','New pH=4.80.')]),('Explain why adding 0.0800 mol NaOH instead would exceed the buffer capacity.',[('R1','It would consume all 0.0600 mol HA and leave strong base in excess, so the weak-acid/conjugate-base buffer pair would no longer remain.')])])
wr('R3.1','ahl','A basic salt solution',r'Sodium ethanoate is dissolved in water. For ethanoic acid $K_a=1.80\times10^{-5}$ and $K_w=1.00\times10^{-14}$ at 298 K. The salt concentration is $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$.',[('Write the hydrolysis equation and calculate Kb for ethanoate.',[('A1','CH₃COO⁻+H₂O⇌CH₃COOH+OH⁻.'),('A1','Kb=Kw/Ka=5.56×10⁻¹⁰.')]),('Estimate [OH⁻] and pH, assuming small hydrolysis.',[('M1','[OH⁻]≈√(Kb c)=√(5.56×10⁻¹¹)=7.45×10⁻⁶ mol dm⁻³.'),('A1','pOH=5.13; pH=14.00−5.13=8.87.')]),('Explain why sodium ions do not account for this basicity.',[('R1','Na⁺ is the ion from a strong base and does not appreciably generate OH⁻ by hydrolysis; ethanoate is the proton-accepting species.')])])
wr('R3.1','ahl','Choosing an indicator from data','A constructed titration curve for a weak monoprotic acid with a strong base has equivalence near pH 8.7 and a steep change spanning pH 7.5–10.0. Available indicators have ranges A: 3.1–4.4, B: 6.0–7.6, C: 8.2–10.0.',[('Select the best indicator and justify your choice.',[('A1','Indicator C.'),('R1','Its transition range lies within the steep region around equivalence, giving the closest volume estimate with little colour-change ambiguity.')]),('Distinguish endpoint from equivalence point and explain why the equivalence pH exceeds 7.',[('R1','The endpoint is the observed indicator colour change; equivalence is the stoichiometric point at which the reacting amounts exactly match.'),('R1','The weak acid’s conjugate base hydrolyses water to form OH⁻, so the salt solution is basic.')]),('Write the indicator equilibrium in general form and state which form increases when OH⁻ is added.',[('A1','HInd⇌H⁺+Ind⁻; removing H⁺ with OH⁻ shifts the equilibrium toward Ind⁻.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R3.2','sl','Balancing a redox titration',r'In acidic solution, $\mathrm{MnO_4^-}$ oxidizes $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ to $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ and is reduced to $\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$.',[('Write the balanced reduction half-equation and the overall ionic equation.',[('A1','MnO₄⁻+8H⁺+5e⁻→Mn²⁺+4H₂O.'),('A1','MnO₄⁻+8H⁺+5Fe²⁺→Mn²⁺+4H₂O+5Fe³⁺.')]),('A 25.00 cm³ Fe²⁺ sample requires 18.40 cm³ of 0.02000 mol dm⁻³ MnO₄⁻. Calculate the Fe²⁺ concentration.',[('M1','n(MnO₄⁻)=0.01840×0.02000=0.0003680 mol.'),('M1','n(Fe²⁺)=5×0.0003680=0.001840 mol.'),('A1','[Fe²⁺]=0.001840/0.02500=0.07360 mol dm⁻³.')])])
wr('R3.2','sl','A zinc–copper cell','A zinc electrode is placed in Zn²⁺ solution and a copper electrode in Cu²⁺ solution. A salt bridge completes the circuit. Zinc is observed to dissolve while copper deposits.',[('Write the two electrode half-equations, identify anode and cathode, and state their signs in this voltaic cell.',[('A1','Zn→Zn²⁺+2e⁻ at the negative anode.'),('A1','Cu²⁺+2e⁻→Cu at the positive cathode.')]),('State the direction of electron flow and explain the salt bridge’s role.',[('A1','Electrons flow from zinc to copper through the external wire.'),('R1','Ions migrate through the bridge to maintain electrical neutrality and complete the internal circuit; electrons do not travel through the salt bridge.')]),('Predict ion migration from an inert KNO₃ bridge.',[('A1','NO₃⁻ migrates toward the zinc compartment, while K⁺ migrates toward the copper compartment.')])])
wr('R3.2','ahl','Cell potential and Gibbs energy',r'A cell reaction is $\mathrm{Zn(s)+Cu^{2+}(aq)\rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq)+Cu(s)}$. Standard reduction potentials are −0.76 V for Zn²⁺/Zn and +0.34 V for Cu²⁺/Cu. Use $F=96500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$.',[('Calculate E°cell and ΔG° for the equation as written.',[('A1','E°cell=0.34−(−0.76)=1.10 V.'),('M1','Two moles of electrons are transferred: ΔG°=−nFE°.'),('A1','ΔG°=−2×96500×1.10=−212 kJ mol⁻¹ of reaction.')]),('If the equation is doubled, state what happens to E°cell and ΔG° and explain.',[('A1','E°cell remains 1.10 V; ΔG° doubles to approximately −425 kJ mol⁻¹ of the doubled reaction.'),('R1','Potential is energy per unit charge and is intensive; both electron amount and total Gibbs change scale with the equation.')])])
wr('R3.2','ahl','Electroplating with a soluble anode','A steel object is copper-plated using aqueous CuSO₄ and a copper anode. Conditions are chosen so copper transfer dominates and gas formation is negligible.',[('State the connection of the object to the power supply and write the cathode and anode half-equations.',[('A1','Connect the object to the negative terminal; it is the cathode.'),('A1','Cathode: Cu²⁺+2e⁻→Cu.'),('A1','Anode: Cu→Cu²⁺+2e⁻.')]),('Explain why the Cu²⁺ concentration remains approximately constant under these assumptions.',[('R1','Cu²⁺ is consumed at the cathode and replenished at the soluble copper anode.'),('R1','The same charge passes both electrodes; with the same two-electron process and near-100% current efficiencies, the amounts produced and consumed are approximately equal.')]),('Explain why replacing the copper anode with an inert electrode would change this concentration balance.',[('R1','An inert anode does not supply Cu²⁺ by dissolving, so copper deposition removes Cu²⁺ without that replenishment.')])])
wr('R3.2','sl','Controlling alcohol oxidation','A primary alcohol, propan-1-ol, is treated with a suitable oxidizing agent. One experiment removes volatile product as it forms; another uses excess oxidant under reflux.',[('Name and give the condensed formula of the main organic product targeted in each experiment.',[('A1','Distillation targets propanal, CH₃CH₂CHO.'),('A1','Reflux with excess oxidant targets propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH.')]),('Explain the different outcomes.',[('R1','Distillation removes the aldehyde from contact with oxidant, limiting further oxidation.'),('R1','Reflux returns volatile material and allows the aldehyde to undergo further oxidation to the acid.')]),('State the main oxidation product of propan-2-ol and explain why the same simple route does not yield propanoic acid.',[('A1','Propanone, CH₃COCH₃.'),('R1','The secondary alcohol forms a ketone; conversion to a carboxylic acid would require carbon–carbon bond cleavage rather than the analogous aldehyde oxidation step.')])])
wr('R3.3','sl','A radical chain for ethane',r'Ethane reacts with chlorine under ultraviolet light to form chloroethane and hydrogen chloride.',[('Write the initiation step and explain the bond-cleavage type.',[('A1','Cl₂→2Cl·, with UV light indicated.'),('R1','Homolytic cleavage sends one electron from the Cl–Cl bond to each atom, giving two radicals.')]),('Write two propagation steps that regenerate a chlorine radical.',[('A1','Cl·+C₂H₆→HCl+C₂H₅·.'),('A1','C₂H₅·+Cl₂→C₂H₅Cl+Cl·.')]),('Write a termination step that forms butane and explain why it is termination.',[('A1','C₂H₅·+C₂H₅·→C₄H₁₀.'),('R1','Two radicals are consumed and no radical is produced.')])])
wr('R3.3','sl','Why a substitution mixture forms','A student photochemically chlorinates propane and expects one pure monochlorinated product.',[('Give the two possible monochlorinated constitutional isomers.',[('A1','1-chloropropane, CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl.'),('A1','2-chloropropane, CH₃CHClCH₃.')]),('Explain two reasons why the actual product mixture may be more complicated than these two substances.',[('R1','Chlorinated products still contain C–H bonds and can undergo further substitution to form multiply chlorinated products.'),('R1','Radicals can combine in different termination steps, including carbon–carbon coupling. Accept another chemically distinct, valid termination-derived product explanation.')]),('Suggest how the relative amount of multiple substitution can be reduced without claiming a perfectly pure product.',[('R1','Use a large excess of propane relative to chlorine, limiting further chlorination of already substituted molecules.')])])
wr('R3.4','ahl','A one-step substitution pathway',r'For the reaction of $\mathrm{CH_3CH_2Br}$ with $\mathrm{OH^-}$, consider an SN2 pathway.',[('Describe the two curly arrows required in the single step, including their origins and destinations.',[('A1','One arrow starts at an oxygen lone pair of OH⁻ and ends at the carbon bonded to Br.'),('A1','The second starts at the C–Br bond and ends on Br, transferring the bonding pair to the leaving group.')]),('Give the products and describe the key features of the transition state.',[('A1','CH₃CH₂OH and Br⁻.'),('R1','The nucleophile attacks opposite the leaving group; C–O bond formation and C–Br bond breaking occur simultaneously with partial bonds in the transition state.')]),('Explain why a free ethyl carbocation should not be drawn as an intermediate in this specified mechanism.',[('R1','SN2 is concerted and has no carbocation intermediate; such an intermediate would describe a different pathway.')])],figure=molecule([(140,170,'HO⁻'),(330,170,'CH₂'),(330,75,'CH₃'),(550,170,'Br')],[(1,2,1),(1,3,1)],'Reactants only; add the electron-pair arrows in your answer'))
wr('R3.4','ahl','A two-step substitution pathway',r'A tertiary halogenoalkane, $(\mathrm{CH_3})_3\mathrm{CBr}$, undergoes substitution by an SN1 route with hydroxide as nucleophile in a simplified mechanism.',[('Show the first step using an equation and describe the required curly arrow.',[('A1',r'$(\mathrm{CH_3})_3\mathrm{CBr}\rightarrow(\mathrm{CH_3})_3\mathrm{C^+}+\mathrm{Br^-}$.'),('A1','Arrow starts at the C–Br bond and ends on Br; the bond cleaves heterolytically.')]),('Describe the subsequent nucleophile attack and give the product.',[('A1','An oxygen lone pair of OH⁻ attacks the positively charged carbon, forming a C–O bond.'),('A1','The product is (CH₃)₃COH, 2-methylpropan-2-ol.')]),('Explain why a tertiary carbocation is generally more stable than a primary carbocation.',[('R1','Alkyl groups donate electron density and help disperse/stabilize the positive charge.'),('R1','A tertiary centre has three alkyl groups providing this stabilization, compared with one at a primary centre.')])])
wr('R3.4','ahl','Regioselectivity in addition',r'Propene reacts with HBr by the usual electrophilic addition mechanism, without a radical initiator.',[('Identify the major product and the more stable carbocation leading to it.',[('A1','2-bromopropane, CH₃CHBrCH₃.'),('A1','The secondary carbocation CH₃–CH⁺–CH₃ is favoured over the primary alternative.')]),('Describe the arrows in the first step and in the bromide-attack step.',[('A1','The π bond donates an electron pair to H of H–Br; the H–Br bond pair moves to Br.'),('A1','A bromide lone pair then forms a bond to the positively charged carbon.')]),('Explain the origin of the major-product preference.',[('R1','The route through the better-stabilized secondary carbocation is favoured; alkyl groups help stabilize the positive charge.')])],figure=molecule([(170,170,'CH₂'),(350,170,'CH'),(560,170,'CH₃')],[(0,1,2),(1,2,1)],'Propene; show addition in your response'))
wr('R3.4','ahl','Electrophilic substitution of benzene',r'Benzene reacts with a supplied electrophile $\mathrm{E^+}$ to form $\mathrm{C_6H_5E}$. You do not need to describe how E⁺ is generated.',[('Describe the electron-pair movement in the electrophile-attack step and the effect on aromatic delocalization.',[('A1','A curly arrow starts from the ring π electron system and ends at E⁺, forming a C–E bond.'),('R1','The intermediate is a positively charged, non-aromatic sigma complex; continuous ring delocalization is temporarily disrupted.')]),('Describe how the final product forms, including the role of a base.',[('A1','A base accepts the proton on the carbon bonded to E.'),('A1','The C–H bond pair returns to the ring to restore the π system and aromatic delocalization.')]),('Explain why substitution is favoured over a final addition product in this model.',[('R1','Substitution restores the stabilizing delocalized aromatic system; a simple addition product would leave that stabilization disrupted.')])])
wr('R3.4','ahl','Complex formation and charge',r'Cobalt(III) forms $[\mathrm{Co(NH_3)_5Cl}]^{2+}$. Treat ammonia as neutral and chloride as −1.',[('Confirm the complex charge and state its coordination number.',[('A1','Total charge=+3+5(0)−1=+2.'),('A1','Coordination number=6, because six donor atoms bond directly to cobalt.')]),('Explain the Lewis acid–base roles during formation of a cobalt–ammonia bond.',[('R1','Ammonia is the Lewis base: its nitrogen lone pair is donated.'),('R1','The cobalt centre is the Lewis acid: it accepts the pair to form a coordinate covalent bond.')]),('State how the charge changes if the coordinated chloride is replaced by a neutral water ligand while cobalt’s oxidation state remains unchanged.',[('A1','The resulting complex has charge +3, because a −1 ligand is replaced by a neutral ligand.')])])
wr('R3.1','ahl','Extracting pKa from titration data','Constructed data are obtained while 25.00 cm³ of a weak monoprotic acid is titrated with 0.1000 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. Equivalence occurs at 25.00 cm³.'+table(['NaOH volume / cm³','pH'],[(5.00,4.20),(10.00,4.62),(12.50,4.80),(20.00,5.40)]),[('Determine pKa and Ka from the half-equivalence point.',[('R1','At half-equivalence [HA]=[A⁻], so [H⁺]=Ka and pH=pKa.'),('A1','pKa=4.80; Ka=10⁻⁴·⁸⁰=1.58×10⁻⁵.')]),('Check whether the 20.00 cm³ reading is consistent with this pKa.',[('M1','Before equivalence, A⁻:HA=20.00:(25.00−20.00)=4:1.'),('A1','pH=4.80+log₁₀4=5.40, consistent with the data.')]),('Explain the approximate effect of doubling the volume of this buffer with water.',[('R1','Both conjugate components are diluted by the same factor, so their ratio and approximate pH remain unchanged; the buffer capacity per unit volume decreases.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(5,4.20),(10,4.62),(12.5,4.80),(20,5.40)],'#186c93')],'NaOH added / cm³','pH',xlim=(0,25),ylim=(3.5,6),title='Constructed buffer-region measurements'))
save('R3')
